WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.115 --> 00:00:02.490
JAMES WEXLER: I'm James, an
engineer on the What-If Tool.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.865
SARA ROBINSON: I'm Sara, a
developer advocate on Cloud AI

00:00:04.865 --> 00:00:06.110
Platform.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:06.510
JAMES WEXLER: We're
going to give you

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.135
an introduction into how you can
use the What-If Tool to better

00:00:09.135 --> 00:00:10.920
understand your machine
learning models.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.337
SARA ROBINSON:
We'll also show you

00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:14.598
how to run it on various
platforms and model types.

00:00:14.598 --> 00:00:17.406
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.332 --> 00:00:22.830
So James, can you tell everyone
what the What-If Tool is

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.300
and when they should use it?

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.220
JAMES WEXLER: The What-If Tool
is a visual interface designed

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.750
to help you understand
your data sets

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.650
and the output of your
machine learning models.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.150
You can run with minimal code
from many different platforms,

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.930
including Jupyter Notebooks,
Kollab, TensorBoard, and Cloud

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.582
AI Platform Notebooks.

00:00:36.582 --> 00:00:38.040
Analysis using the
What-If Tool can

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.498
be helpful during training data
collection, model creation,

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:42.330
and post-training evaluation.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.970
Sara, you've tried the What-If
Tool on a bunch platforms.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.630
What are the ways
you can use it?

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.450
SARA ROBINSON: The What-If Tool
supports TensorFlow models out

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.100
of the box and
can support models

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.375
built with any other framework
with just a few lines of code.

00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:54.750
You can also use
the What-If Tool

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.453
with your models deployed
on Cloud AI Platform.

00:00:57.453 --> 00:00:59.620
JAMES WEXLER: Let's start
with an example in Kollab.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.037
Here we've trained a TensorFlow
model on this Iowa Housing

00:01:02.037 --> 00:01:03.120
data set from Kaggle.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.590
It's a binary
classification model

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.540
that predicts whether
a house is worth more

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.670
or less than $160,000.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.240
We can instantiate the What-If
Tool with a few lines of code

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.550
by passing it a set
of test data points

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.597
along with the ground truth
labels for those examples.

00:01:18.597 --> 00:01:20.930
The What-If Tool has three
tabs with different features.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.555
And the initial view
we're dropped into

00:01:22.555 --> 00:01:24.693
is the data point editor.

00:01:24.693 --> 00:01:27.110
Here we can see our model's
prediction for each data point

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.475
we've sent to the What-If Tool.

00:01:28.475 --> 00:01:30.290
The blue points indicate
houses our model

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.630
classified as above $160,000.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.780
In the data point
editor, we can also

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.420
inspect individual data
points, change feature values,

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.880
create custom visualizations,
and much more.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.130
SARA ROBINSON: The next tab,
Performance and Fairness,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.780
shows a couple of
common evaluation

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.410
metrics for our model.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.470
It also lets us slice
data by different features

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.010
and apply various strategies
to optimize for fairness.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.320
The final tab,
Features, shows us

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.060
how balanced our data set is.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.970
For each feature, we can see
the range of values for the data

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.740
we've sent to the What-If Tool.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.530
You can load the Features
tab with only a data set even

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.472
before you've trained a model.

00:02:05.472 --> 00:02:06.680
JAMES WEXLER: That's awesome.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.570
Do you want to show how you can
use the What-If Tool for models

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.358
deployed on Cloud AI Platform?

00:02:10.358 --> 00:02:11.150
SARA ROBINSON: Yes.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:14.340
Here are a few models I've
deployed on AI Platform.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.730
I'll open up an AI Platform
Notebook instance here

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.820
to analyze this XGBoost model.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.120
Then I create an instance of the
Wit Config Builder, this time

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.550
using the set AI
Platform model method,

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.690
passing in my Google Cloud
project ID, model name,

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.230
and model version.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.150
The Wit Config Builder
contains more methods

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.270
to customize the What-If
Tool for your use case.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.020
JAMES WEXLER: I noticed
two of your models

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.100
are trained on
the same data set.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.300
Can you show us how
we can use the What-If

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.530
Tool to compare them?

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:40.850
SARA ROBINSON: Sure.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.280
All I need to do is add a
line to my Wit Config Builder

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.680
to tell it which model
I want to compare.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.600
Now you can compare
the performance

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.780
of both models using various
capabilities in the What-If

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.423
Tool.

00:02:52.423 --> 00:02:53.840
I should also
mention that you can

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.150
use the What-If Tool for
both classification models

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.222
and regression models.

00:02:57.222 --> 00:02:59.180
JAMES WEXLER: So to recap,
we've introduced you

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.520
to using the What-If Tool on
various platforms and model

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.270
types.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.770
Stay tuned for more
videos to learn

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.437
what you can do in
the data point editor

00:03:05.437 --> 00:03:07.800
and Performance and
Fairness tabs of the tool.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.092
SARA ROBINSON: You can also
check out more What-If Tool

00:03:10.092 --> 00:03:11.960
demos in the links below
to see how it works

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.620
with image and text models.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.380
Thanks for watching.

